A||||8||Mach10||Odd noise from dashboard||||5||
Z||000000||Mach10||05-12-2001||01:36 AM||||Can't figure this one out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">.<BR>There is a weird humming noise coming from the dashboard. Seems to come and go, and stays more or less constant, unless I touch the heater/vent controls. Then it changes a little, and remains constant. If I hit the A/C, it changes quite a bit, but is still present. At the same time, I had a few drops of an oily yellow liquid drip out from underneath the passenger side dash. Nothing else. I am SO stumped...<BR>The AC does not work. I think it has no refridgerant. The sound is there (when it is there) regardless of what I do to the controls. It *MAY* disappear after I turn off the car, but will usually come back on the next startup.<BR>Weird. Anyone have experience with this?<BR>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||05-12-2001||10:09 AM||||Sounds like your heater core and blower motor are both shot.<P>Heater core is the source of your "yellow liquid" and I believe GM blower motors run all the time even when the control is in the "off" position. If the bearing on the blower motor is bad, it will usually make more noise on the low speed. Another possibility is you have debris in the fan housing and you're getting a "card in the bike spokes" type sound.<P>Do a search and you'll find more info on replacing the heater core. I have no idea how difficult the blower motor is to replace. I think you can take the blower resistor off in the front compartment and possibly take a look inside the fan housing to see if anything is in there.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||05-12-2001||02:44 PM||||Well, I didn't know that about the blower motor. That might explain the noise. Thing is though, that the noise doesn't get louder when you crank up the fan speed, nor does it go up in pitch. <P>As for the yellow fluid, it's not engine coolant, as would be evident in a blown heater core. Seems more like a light oil. Actually, do the blower motors have sealed bearings? Cos if they do, I may have just blown one   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 05-12-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||Mach10||05-12-2001||09:08 PM||||*bump*||||reg||6
Z||000004||sjp777||05-12-2001||09:43 PM||||Usually a blower motor with bearings going bad will only make noise at lower speeds. If you turn it up the noise goes away.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||daved||05-13-2001||12:34 AM||||Another thing to check would be the heater duct mode doors.  These are driven by small motors that may have a problem. Try removing the HTR-A/C Fuse (25A).  If the sound goes away, then you will know it is in the heater.  If you have a Haynes, see the diagram on page 296.  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||05-13-2001||02:24 AM||||Sorry, don't have a haynes. Have a Chilton, which is MUCH better for instructions, but is somewhat lacking in diagrams... Oh well, I never looked at the pictures anyway. Come to think of it, I rarely follow directions either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks for the tips. I'll pull the damn thing apart tommorrow. I still can't explain the yellow oil.<P>Are there any air conditioning fittings under the passenger-side dashboard? I'm thinking that if I have a large leak there, it'd explain the oil AND the lack of air-con functionality  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||Wolfhound||05-13-2001||08:27 AM||||The blower motor is accessed through the front hood. It's mounted through to the back side of the dash and easy to remove and check.||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||05-13-2001||02:05 PM||||That oil is most likely refrigerant oil.||||reg||1
